<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</h1><p class="st-meta">1945 CE – 1992 CE · capital Belgrade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tito's communist federation of six South Slav republics, which broke with Stalin, led the Non-Aligned Movement and broke apart in war in the 1990s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-partisans">Partisans</a> emerged victorious from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> after defeating occupiers, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chetniks">Chetniks</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-council-for-the-national-liberation-of-yugoslavia">AVNOJ</a> council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> in 1943 had set out a federal state, Democratic Federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> was formed in 1945, and on 29 November 1945 the monarchy was abolished and a federal people's republic proclaimed, with six republics.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito's</a> split with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a> in 1948, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> developed its own socialism based on workers' self-management and market elements, and in 1961 co-founded the Non-Aligned Movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. Its citizens could travel and work abroad. The 1974 constitution decentralised power to the republics and autonomous provinces, while Tito ruled as president for life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito's</a> death in 1980, debt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and nationalism grew, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> declared independence in 1991, followed by Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, amid war. In April 1992 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia-and-montenegro">Serbia and Montenegro</a> formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia.jpg" alt="Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Flag designed by Đorđe Andrejević-Kun[3]SVG coding: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Yugoslavia_(1946-1992).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1945 CE – 1992 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belgrade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Josip Broz Tito, Ante Marković</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbo-Croatian, Slovene, Macedonian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>SFRY, Federal People's Republic of Yugoslavia, Democratic Federal Yugoslavia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1968&amp;focus=socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia" aria-label="Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 594 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5488 850L5488 850M5488 850L5378 819M5488 850L5488 850M5488 850L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0M5378 819h0M5488 850h0M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5488 850h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Belgrade. <a href="/atlas?year=1968&amp;focus=socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
